---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
---

<DocsLayout title="Primitive Types — IonPath" current="/guides/primitive-types/">
  <div class="animate-fade-in-up">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight mb-2">Primitive Types</h1>
    <p class="text-text-secondary mb-8">IonPath provides a rich set of built-in types covering integers, floats, strings, dates, and special types. Each type has a fixed wire encoding and mappings to C# and TypeScript.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="space-y-12 animate-fade-in-up-delay-1">

    <!-- Integer Types -->
    <section>
      <h2 id="integers" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Integer Types</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4">Signed and unsigned integers from 8-bit to 128-bit.</p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-oled-border-subtle">
              <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-text-muted font-medium">Ion Type</th>
              <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-text-muted font-medium">C#</th>
              <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-text-muted font-medium">TypeScript</th>
              <th class="text-center py-2 px-3 text-text-muted font-medium">Size</th>
              <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-text-muted font-medium">Description</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-text-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-oled-border-subtle"><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono type-keyword">i1</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">sbyte</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">number</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-center">8 bits</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-xs">Signed byte (-128 to 127)</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-oled-border-subtle"><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono type-keyword">i2</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">short</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">number</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-center">16 bits</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-xs">Signed short</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-oled-border-subtle"><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono type-keyword">i4</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">int</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">number</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-center">32 bits</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-xs">Signed integer</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-oled-border-subtle"><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono type-keyword">i8</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">long</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">bigint</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-center">64 bits</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-xs">Signed long</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-oled-border-subtle"><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono type-keyword">i16</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">Int128</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">bigint</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-center">128 bits</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-xs">Signed 128-bit integer</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-oled-border-subtle"><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono type-keyword">u1</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">byte</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">number</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-center">8 bits</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-xs">Unsigned byte (0 to 255)</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-oled-border-subtle"><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono type-keyword">u2</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">ushort</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">number</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-center">16 bits</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-xs">Unsigned short</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-oled-border-subtle"><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono type-keyword">u4</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">uint</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">number</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-center">32 bits</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-xs">Unsigned integer</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-oled-border-subtle"><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono type-keyword">u8</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">ulong</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">bigint</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-center">64 bits</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-xs">Unsigned long</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono type-keyword">u16</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">UInt128</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">bigint</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-center">128 bits</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-xs">Unsigned 128-bit integer</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="mt-4 flex items-start gap-3 rounded-xl border border-oled-border bg-oled-surface/50 p-4">
        <span class="text-accent text-sm mt-0.5">ℹ</span>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">The naming convention follows <strong>byte count</strong>: <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">i4</code> = 4 bytes = 32 bits. <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">u</code> prefix means unsigned, <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">i</code> means signed.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Float Types -->
    <section>
      <h2 id="floats" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Floating-Point Types</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-oled-border-subtle">
              <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-text-muted font-medium">Ion Type</th>
              <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-text-muted font-medium">C#</th>
              <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-text-muted font-medium">TypeScript</th>
              <th class="text-center py-2 px-3 text-text-muted font-medium">Size</th>
              <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-text-muted font-medium">Description</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-text-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-oled-border-subtle"><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono type-keyword">f2</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">Half</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">number</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-center">16 bits</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-xs">IEEE 754 half precision</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-oled-border-subtle"><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono type-keyword">f4</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">float</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">number</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-center">32 bits</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-xs">IEEE 754 single precision</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono type-keyword">f8</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">double</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">number</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-center">64 bits</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-xs">IEEE 754 double precision</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="mt-4 rounded-lg border border-oled-border-subtle bg-oled-elevated p-4">
        <p class="text-sm font-medium mb-2">Float wire encoding: the declared width is always written</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">
          A float is encoded at exactly the width its Ion type declares — <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">f2</code> as CBOR
          <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">0xf9</code> + 2 bytes,
          <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">f4</code> as
          <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">0xfa</code> + 4 bytes,
          <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">f8</code> as
          <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">0xfb</code> + 8 bytes — regardless of the value. IonPath deliberately does
          <em>not</em> use CBOR's shortest-lossless float form: Ion is schema-first, so the reader already knows the width, and shrinking would make
          <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">f2</code> and
          <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">f4</code> indistinguishable on the wire. This makes a given message byte-identical
          across the C#, TypeScript and Rust runtimes, which matters for hashing, signing and content-addressing.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-2">
          Readers accept any of the three widths for any float field, so payloads written by older releases — which did shrink — still decode. <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">NaN</code>
          is normalized to positive quiet NaN on write; <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">-0.0</code>, subnormals and the
          infinities are preserved bit-for-bit.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- decimal -->
    <section>
      <h2 id="decimal" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Exact Decimal</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4">A base-10 number that is not a binary float. Use it for money, for tax rates, and for anything else where <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">0.1 + 0.2 != 0.3</code> is a bug rather than a curiosity.</p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-oled-border-subtle">
              <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-text-muted font-medium">Ion Type</th>
              <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-text-muted font-medium">C#</th>
              <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-text-muted font-medium">TypeScript</th>
              <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-text-muted font-medium">Rust</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-text-secondary">
            <tr><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono type-keyword">decimal</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">System.Decimal</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">IonDecimal</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">ion_rustcore::IonDecimal</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="mt-4 rounded-lg border border-oled-border-subtle bg-oled-elevated p-4">
        <p class="text-sm font-medium mb-2">Wire encoding: CBOR tag 4</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">
          A <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">decimal</code> is CBOR <strong>tag 4</strong> — the standard decimal-fraction tag — wrapping a
          two element array <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">[exponent, mantissa]</code>, giving the value
          <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">mantissa × 10^exponent</code>. It is deliberately <em>not</em> mapped to
          <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">f8</code>: the entire point of the type is that it does not lose precision.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-2">
          <strong>The mantissa is normalised on write</strong>, so <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">1.50</code> and
          <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">1.5</code> produce the same bytes and a value cannot carry a trailing-zero "scale" across the
          wire. <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">-0.0m</code> and <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">0m</code> also
          normalise to the same encoding — the one deliberate place in the language where two distinguishable source values become one wire value.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-2">
          Tag 4 permits an arbitrary mantissa and exponent, but the C# runtime type does not: a payload outside
          <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">System.Decimal</code>'s 96-bit magnitude or 0..28 scale is a typed range error on read rather than a
          silently truncated value. Not a Map key — see <a href="/guides/collections/#map-keys" class="text-accent hover:underline">Collections</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Special Types -->
    <section>
      <h2 id="special" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Special Types</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-oled-border-subtle">
              <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-text-muted font-medium">Ion Type</th>
              <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-text-muted font-medium">C#</th>
              <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-text-muted font-medium">TypeScript</th>
              <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-text-muted font-medium">Description</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-text-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-oled-border-subtle"><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono type-keyword">void</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">void</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">void</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-xs">No value — used for methods with no return</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-oled-border-subtle"><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono type-keyword">bool</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">bool</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">boolean</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-xs">Boolean true/false</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-oled-border-subtle"><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono type-keyword">string</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">string</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">string</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-xs">UTF-8 text</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-oled-border-subtle"><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono type-keyword">guid</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">Guid</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">string</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-xs">128-bit UUID</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-oled-border-subtle"><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono type-keyword">bigint</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">BigInteger</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">bigint</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-xs">Arbitrary-precision integer</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-oled-border-subtle"><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono type-keyword">bytes</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">byte[] / IonBytes</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">Uint8Array</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-xs">Raw binary data</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono type-keyword">uri</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">Uri</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">string</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-xs">URI/URL</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Date/Time Types -->
    <section>
      <h2 id="datetime" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Date & Time Types</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-oled-border-subtle">
              <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-text-muted font-medium">Ion Type</th>
              <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-text-muted font-medium">C#</th>
              <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-text-muted font-medium">TypeScript</th>
              <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-text-muted font-medium">Rust</th>
              <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-text-muted font-medium">Description</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-text-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-oled-border-subtle"><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono type-keyword">datetime</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">DateTimeOffset</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">IonDateTime</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">chrono::DateTime&lt;FixedOffset&gt;</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-xs">Instant with an explicit UTC offset</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-oled-border-subtle"><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono type-keyword">dateonly</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">DateOnly</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">DateOnly</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">IonDateOnly</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-xs">Date without time component</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-oled-border-subtle"><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono type-keyword">timeonly</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">TimeOnly</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">TimeOnly</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">IonTimeOnly</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-xs">Time without date component</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono type-keyword">duration</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">TimeSpan</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">Duration</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">IonDuration</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-xs">Time span / duration</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-6 rounded-lg border border-status-down/40 bg-oled-elevated p-4">
        <p class="text-sm font-medium mb-2"><span class="text-status-down">⚠</span> <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">datetime</code> changed on the wire — and this is the fix, not a regression</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">
          The old encoding was <strong>not interoperable</strong>. The three runtimes wrote three different shapes and no two of them fully agreed:
        </p>
        <ul class="text-sm text-text-secondary space-y-2 mt-3 ml-4">
          <li>• <strong>C#</strong> wrote tag 0 + RFC 3339 text, then read it back through <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">.UtcDateTime</code> — parsing the offset and <em>throwing it away</em>. A value authored at <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">+05:30</code> came back as UTC.</li>
          <li>• <strong>TypeScript</strong> wrote <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Date.toISOString()</code>, which is millisecond resolution, so the 100ns ticks a C# writer produced were silently truncated on every exchange.</li>
          <li>• <strong>Rust</strong> wrote a bare CBOR array <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">[ticks, offset_minutes]</code> — a different CBOR major type entirely. <strong>Rust and C# could not exchange a <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">datetime</code> at all.</strong></li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-4 rounded-lg border border-oled-border-subtle bg-oled-elevated p-4">
        <p class="text-sm font-medium mb-2">The encoding now</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">
          CBOR <strong>tag 0</strong> wrapping an RFC 3339 date-time, <strong>always with an explicit numeric offset</strong> and
          <strong>always with exactly seven fractional-second digits</strong>:
        </p>
        <div class="code-block text-[13px] mt-3">
          <pre><span class="text-accent-2">"2024-03-01T12:34:56.7891234+05:30"</span>   <span class="text-text-muted">// 33 characters, 36 wire bytes: c0 78 21 …</span></pre>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3">
          <strong>Seven digits, not fewer and not more.</strong> 100ns is the resolution of the most precise runtime type in play, the .NET tick. Fewer
          would lose C# precision on the wire; more would be unrepresentable in C# and so could never be written back byte-identically.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-2">
          <strong>A numeric offset, never <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Z</code>.</strong>
          <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Z</code> and <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">+00:00</code> denote the same
          instant but are different bytes, so exactly one has to be canonical. Writers pick <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">+00:00</code>
          because it removes the special case: every offset is formatted the same way.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-2">
          <strong>Readers are lenient.</strong> Any fractional precision from 0 to 9 digits is accepted, with digits past the seventh
          <em>truncated</em> rather than rounded — rounding <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">.99999995</code> would carry into the next second
          and no two runtimes would agree on the carry at every boundary. <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Z</code> is accepted alongside a
          numeric offset, the <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">T</code> separator may be lower case or a space, and an untagged text string
          is accepted because the schema already says the field is a <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">datetime</code>. A missing offset is
          <em>rejected</em>: a local time without one is ambiguous and is not assumed to be UTC.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="glass rounded-xl p-4 mt-4 border-l-2 border-status-degraded/40">
        <p class="text-sm font-medium mb-1">Migration</p>
        <p class="text-xs text-text-secondary">Payloads written by an older release do not decode reliably: a Rust-written value is a CBOR array where a tag-0 text string is now expected, and a C#-written value loses its offset. There is no compatibility shim — the old format had no single definition to be compatible <em>with</em>. Redeploy peers together. In C#, a <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">datetime</code> field is now <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">System.DateTimeOffset</code> rather than <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">System.DateTime</code>, which is a source-breaking change your compiler will point at; in TypeScript it is <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">IonDateTime</code> rather than <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Date</code>, with <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">IonDateTime.fromDate()</code> to convert.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Generic Wrappers -->
    <section>
      <h2 id="wrappers" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Built-in Generics</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4">Five built-in generics wrap or collect other types. The first three have a suffix shorthand; <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Map</code> and <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Set</code> do not and are always written out. See <a href="/guides/collections/" class="text-accent hover:underline">Collections</a> for the wire formats and the key restrictions.</p>
      <div class="type-grid">
        <div class="type-grid-header">
          <span>Wrapper</span>
          <span>Description</span>
        </div>
        <div class="type-grid-row">
          <span class="font-mono text-[12px]"><span class="type-name">Maybe</span>&lt;<span class="type-keyword">T</span>&gt;</span>
          <span class="text-[12px] text-text-secondary">Optional value. Shorthand: <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">T?</code>. Null on the wire when absent.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="type-grid-row">
          <span class="font-mono text-[12px]"><span class="type-name">Array</span>&lt;<span class="type-keyword">T</span>&gt;</span>
          <span class="text-[12px] text-text-secondary">Variable-length sequence. Shorthand: <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">T[]</code>. CBOR array encoding.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="type-grid-row">
          <span class="font-mono text-[12px]"><span class="type-name">Partial</span>&lt;<span class="type-keyword">T</span>&gt;</span>
          <span class="text-[12px] text-text-secondary">Sparse patch — only touched fields are serialized. Shorthand: <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">T~</code>.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="type-grid-row">
          <span class="font-mono text-[12px]"><span class="type-name">Map</span>&lt;<span class="type-keyword">K</span>, <span class="type-keyword">V</span>&gt;</span>
          <span class="text-[12px] text-text-secondary">Keyed collection. <strong>No shorthand.</strong> A definite-length CBOR map with keys in canonical order; keys are restricted.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="type-grid-row">
          <span class="font-mono text-[12px]"><span class="type-name">Set</span>&lt;<span class="type-keyword">T</span>&gt;</span>
          <span class="text-[12px] text-text-secondary">Distinct collection. <strong>No shorthand.</strong> CBOR tag 258 over a sorted array.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-4">A fixed-size array is written <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">T[N]</code> and has no spelled-out generic form in source; it records in the lock as <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Array&lt;T, N&gt;</code>.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- Usage Example -->
    <section>
      <h2 id="usage" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Usage Example</h2>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-3">msg</span> <span class="type-name">UserProfile</span> {'{'}
    id: <span class="type-keyword">u4</span>;                  <span class="text-text-muted">// unsigned 32-bit integer</span>
    name: <span class="type-keyword">string</span>;             <span class="text-text-muted">// UTF-8 text</span>
    email: <span class="type-keyword">string</span>?;            <span class="text-text-muted">// optional string (Maybe&lt;string&gt;)</span>
    avatar: <span class="type-keyword">bytes</span>?;            <span class="text-text-muted">// optional binary data</span>
    score: <span class="type-keyword">f8</span>;                <span class="text-text-muted">// double-precision float</span>
    balance: <span class="type-keyword">decimal</span>;          <span class="text-text-muted">// exact base-10 — not a binary float</span>
    createdAt: <span class="type-keyword">datetime</span>;       <span class="text-text-muted">// instant + explicit offset</span>
    tags: <span class="type-keyword">string</span>[];            <span class="text-text-muted">// array of strings</span>
    largeId: <span class="type-keyword">u8</span>;              <span class="text-text-muted">// 64-bit unsigned (bigint in TS)</span>
    uniqueRef: <span class="type-keyword">guid</span>;          <span class="text-text-muted">// 128-bit UUID</span>
{'}'}</pre>
      </div>
    </section>

  </div>
